UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 4, Week 21, Lesson 2: Writing in role', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Writing in role means using a character’s voice, knowledge and feelings consistently, without leaking narrator facts the character could not know.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same strategy to an unfamiliar topic or audience, then compare the effect of two different choices.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nRewrite a third-person line into first-person role for a nervous new student on day one.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUse I and details that student would notice; avoid all-knowing narrator comments.\n\nCHECK\nBreaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nWrite a 140–160 word in-role diary or letter from a story character. Stay consistent and explain one voice choice.\n\nWrite a short reflection describing one specific revision you made and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"English","learning_intention":"Writing in role means using a character’s voice, knowledge and feelings consistently, without leaking narrator facts the character could not know.","lesson_number":2,"lesson_role":"Writing","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["EN2-CWT-01","EN2-UARL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Write a 140–160 word in-role diary or letter from a story character. Stay consistent and explain one voice choice. Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using in role on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective.. Correction: Use I and details that student would notice;. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating in role on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with in role, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about in role with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for in role. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for in role. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective","A claim that names no evidence from the lesson material","A method that skips the key step taught in this lesson","Use I and details that student would notice"],"question":"Rewrite a third-person line into first-person role for a nervous new student on day one. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for in role; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective","Writing in role means using a character’s voice, knowledge and feelings consistently, without leaking narrator facts","Use I and details that student would notice","Careful use of voice and explain why it matters with clear evidence"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with in role?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named voice and explain why it matters, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name voice and explain why it matters only, without linking it to in role","Apply in role to new material and name the voice and explain why it matters used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence","Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective"],"question":"Which response best applies in role with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The skill is shown by quoting or pointing to wording that carries in role. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Say the writing is good without naming any words","Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective","Point to exact words in the sentence that show in role and say what they do","Ignore the sentence and invent a more exciting event"],"question":"Read: \"After the blackout, Tess lit a candle and read the crumpled note.\" Which choice best uses in role from this lesson?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"The correct statement applies the language skill with textual evidence. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Name voice and explain why it matters without linking it to any words in the text","Use I and details that student would notice","Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective"],"question":"Which statement correctly applies the lesson idea about in role?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Start from a specific textual clue, then explain its effect. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Guess from the title alone with no textual clue","Copy a model sentence from another story unchanged","Lengthen the answer with adjectives while keeping the same idea","Locate a specific word, phrase or structural clue before deciding"],"question":"In a new text, what should you do first to analyse voice and explain why it matters?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Repair means fixing meaning or evidence, not padding. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change the inaccurate idea or missing evidence, not only the length","Add adjectives without fixing the wrong idea","Make the answer longer while keeping the same mistake","Delete the evidence that disagreed with your first guess"],"question":"Which revision actually improves meaning for in role?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Accurate transfer needs fresh material with an explained language choice. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Change fonts and claim the work is new","Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective","Use a fresh sentence or text and explain one precise choice about in role","Retype the worked model as the final answer"],"question":"Which response shows accurate use of in role on fresh material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about in role with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating in role on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using in role on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for in role. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with in role, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective.. Correction: Use I and details that student would notice;. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent task and reflection, with explanations where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s2-eng-y4-w21-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Proposed Stage 2 NESA alignment; verify content points before acceptance.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S2","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise in role.","Read the mission before you start writing or speaking.","Keep the audience and purpose in mind as you work."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your reason with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the evidence."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the wording that carries the meaning.","Then explain the choice without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore the example.","Prefer evidence over wording that merely sounds clever."],"submit":["Check that your response and reason are both present.","Proofread names, sequence and punctuation.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact word or action before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and underline one useful detail.","Say what that detail helps a reader understand.","A vague compliment is weaker than quoted evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak attempt with a stronger revision.","Change meaning or accuracy, not only length.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh topic that still uses the same skill.","Explain one decision in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include an original response and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new content."]},"subject":"English","success_criteria":["I can use in role with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify voice and explain why it matters.","I can practise character independently on a new prompt.","I can notice perspective needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Writing in role means using a character’s voice, knowledge and feelings consistently, without leaking narrator facts the character could not know.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Writing in role means using a character’s voice, knowledge and feelings consistently, without leaking narrator facts","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about in role with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIn role as the ferry attendant: \"I counted tickets twice before we cast off.\" I do not mention a secret the attendant never learned.\n\nTRY FIRST\nRewrite a third-person line into first-person role for a nervous new student on day one.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUse I and details that student would notice; avoid all-knowing narrator comments.","explain":"Writing in role means using a character’s voice, knowledge and feelings consistently, without leaking narrator facts the character could not know.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"Consistent in-role voice.","caption":"Stay inside the character’s knowledge.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">In role</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">I counted tickets…</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Leak risk</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">facts character cannot know</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: In role as the ferry attendant: \"I counted tickets twice before we cast off.\" I do not mention a secret the attendant ne Then write your own attempt.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which detail matters most.\nJuno: Name the exact words or actions that carry the meaning before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: In role as the ferry attendant: \"I counted tickets twice before we cast off.\" I do not mention a secret the attendant ne Then write your own attempt."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.","options":["A guess with no explanation","A claim unrelated to the text","Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about in role with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nIn role as the ferry attendant: \"I counted tickets twice before we cast off.\" I do not mention a secret the attendant never learned. Think about how this word or relationship creates meaning.\n\nTRY FIRST\nUnderline or quote one precise detail in the model and explain its effect.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful evidence is a specific choice in the example, followed by an explanation of the effect on meaning.","explain":"Make your choice visible: select the exact detail in the model that demonstrates the skill, and describe what it does for a reader.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"Saying a choice is good without naming the actual detail does not show understanding. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Explain the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"First-person role contrasted with narrator leak.","caption":"Explain one voice choice.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Voice</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">nervous day-one I</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Break</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">all-knowing aside</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison.","options":["Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about in role with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nBreaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective. Here is a more productive model: In role as the ferry attendant: \"I counted tickets twice before we cast off.\" I do not mention a secret the attendant never learned.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against approach would weaken your work, then write a corrected version.\n\nFEEDBACK\nUse I and details that student would notice; avoid all-knowing narrator comments.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect attempt as a comparison. Name its specific error, then show how a revision fixes that error.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else. Keep the meaning accurate and explain the choice.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the original example and locate the words or actions that support the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Check your response against the feedback: did you address the same skill and show your reasoning? If a detail is uncertain, identify what would have to be checked. One deliberate, justified revision is more useful than making the answer longer.","icon":"📖","notice":"A superficial change in length or appearance does not repair meaning. Breaking character with out-of-role knowledge confuses perspective. Correct the meaning, not just the appearance.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example.","options":["Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example","A claim unrelated to the text","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no explanation"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about in role with clear evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nWriting in role means using a character’s voice, knowledge and feelings consistently, without leaking narrator facts the character could not know. Now choose different people, facts or text to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nMake one fresh example demonstrating the skill and explain your main choice.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should meet the same purpose as the model, but contain original material.","explain":"Use the method on a new topic, not a reworded copy of the example. Review the result for accuracy and reader clarity.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner make decisions based on specific language evidence, rather than guess or copy someone else